---
import BaseLayout from "../../../shared/components/BaseLayout.astro";
import TypeBadge from "../../../shared/components/TypeBadge.astro";
import { MAIN_SITE_URL, DATA_SITE_URL } from "../../../shared/config/config";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebPage",
  name: "Methodology",
  url: MAIN_SITE_URL + "/methodology/",
  description: "How this platform produces content: the four-layer content system, de-identification rules, evidence standards, and quality gates.",
  isPartOf: { "@type": "WebSite", "name": "Porn Recovery Knowledge Platform", "url": "https://selfcontrolatlas.com" },
};

---
<BaseLayout
  siteTitle="Porn Recovery Knowledge Platform"
  siteUrl={MAIN_SITE_URL}
  title="Methodology — How Content Is Produced and Labeled"
  description="How this platform produces content: the four-layer content system, de-identification rules, evidence standards, and quality gates."
  canonical="/methodology/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Home", url: "/" }, { name: "Methodology", url: "/methodology/" }]}
>
  <h1>Methodology</h1>
  <p>This platform combines two different kinds of information that must never be
  confused: what people report experiencing, and what scientific research has found.</p>

  <h2>The four content layers</h2>
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Label</th><th>What it contains</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1. Personal Experience</td><td><TypeBadge type="experience" /></td>
        <td>De-identified, structured reports from community members</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2. Community Pattern</td><td><TypeBadge type="pattern" /></td>
        <td>Observations that recur across many independent experiences</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3. Scientific Evidence</td><td><TypeBadge type="research" /></td>
        <td>Peer-reviewed research, systematic reviews, clinical guidelines</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4. Clinical / Professional Info</td><td><TypeBadge type="clinical" /></td>
        <td>Information from professional and clinical sources</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Uncertain</td><td><TypeBadge type="uncertain" /></td>
        <td>Topics where evidence is missing, mixed, or debated</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Where community experiences come from</h2>
  <p>Experiences are extracted from publicly accessible community discussions (for
  example, Chinese-language recovery communities such as Jiese Ba). Extraction follows
  strict rules:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>De-identification</strong> — no usernames, IDs, avatars, contact info, or locations</li>
    <li><strong>Redaction</strong> — sexual details are reduced to what is necessary to understand the experience</li>
    <li><strong>Rewrite</strong> — long posts are condensed into structured records, never copied verbatim</li>
    <li><strong>Labeling</strong> — every record is labeled as community-reported personal experience</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Evidence standards</h2>
  <p>Scientific content is sourced independently from community experience, using
  authoritative tiers: WHO / ICD-11, systematic reviews and meta-analyses,
  peer-reviewed journals, and professional organizations. Community material is never
  used as scientific evidence. When evidence is missing or mixed, the site says so.</p>

  <h2>Data interpretation framework</h2>
  <p>All statistics and pattern analyses built on this platform's experience dataset are
  interpreted under explicit constraints:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sample size and source population.</strong> Statistics report the number of
    processed records; counts are stated plainly (for example, "62 of 124 records").</li>
    <li><strong>Selection bias.</strong> People post when they relapse or reach milestones;
    the dataset is self-selected and cannot represent any general population.</li>
    <li><strong>Reporting bias.</strong> Posts reflect what people choose to share, not a
    complete account of their experience.</li>
    <li><strong>Survivorship bias.</strong> Success stories are more visible than quiet
    non-posters; absence from the dataset is not evidence of absence.</li>
    <li><strong>Coding methodology.</strong> Records are manually structured from source
    posts with de-identification; counts reflect records mentioning each item, not
    prevalence in any population.</li>
    <li><strong>What frequency means.</strong> A frequency count means "this was mentioned
    often," nothing more.</li>
    <li><strong>What frequency does NOT mean.</strong> It does not mean the item is common,
    effective, causal, or important.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>In short: <strong>community dataset ≠ general population</strong>, and
  <strong>community experience ≠ clinical evidence</strong>. Analysis pieces repeat these
  constraints and label opinions as opinions.</p>

  <h2>Research process</h2>
  <p>The evidence layer is built by locating published studies through academic databases,
  recording source identifiers (DOI/PMID where verified), and summarizing them with
  explicit evidence-strength ratings. Where a source identifier cannot be verified, it is
  left unstated rather than guessed. Summaries are paraphrases, not reproductions of
  publisher text. See the <a href={DATA_SITE_URL + "/evidence/"}>evidence standards</a>.</p>

  <h2>What this platform is not</h2>
  <p>This is not a medical site, not a diagnosis tool, and not a treatment program.
  Content does not establish that any specific strategy "works" — it reports what
  people tried and what evidence exists.</p>
</BaseLayout>
